+# Basic tiered SkyCache experiment — 29 October 2026
+
+This patch reduces repeated work for a workspace whose source snapshot is held
+immutable by its caller. It uses the existing retained Skyframe graph, local
+publications and remote publications. It does not implement a new cache protocol.
+
+Base: `a23ae68903f03f08ce83dcf1be85cdff54bb1eeb` on
+`perf/skycache-analysis-foundation`. The transferable patch is packaged separately.
+No benchmark has been run for this change; this document makes no speedup claim.
+
+## Scope and contract
+
+The new optional flag is:
+
+```text
+--experimental_local_skycache_immutable_workspace=<opaque-snapshot-identity>
+```
+
+The caller must enforce that every use of an identity sees the same workspace
+source contents, directory membership, missing paths, modes and symlink resolution.
+This includes relevant untracked and ignored files. The option does not freeze the
+filesystem, inspect Git cleanliness or make a Git commit ID sufficient evidence.
+Reusing an identity after changing these inputs violates the contract and can
+produce stale hits. Use a different identity for a different immutable snapshot;
+leave the option empty for normal mutable-workspace validation.
+
+The identity is incorporated into the cache namespace, with a separate immutable
+workspace format tag. Different identities, normal mutable mode, and immutable
+mode therefore cannot share these workspace proofs. Existing namespace isolation
+for repository identity, native byte order and effective environment remains.
+Existing configuration/serialization key checks also remain. This is not portable
+sharing between independent clones or machines.
+
+`LocalInputSnapshot` uses the asserted identity instead of recursively hashing the
+whole workspace at startup and finalization. For eligible workspace witnesses it
+checks canonical evidence and compatibility with evidence already consumed by
+this command, without rereading the source bytes. Path validation still occurs.
+Workspace-root observations, `.git` and top-level `bazel-*` paths retain normal
+checks; the root listing can include mutable Git administration and Bazel
+convenience links.
+
+External and absolute-path witnesses, external repository bindings, and physical
+referents outside the workspace remain validated normally. Dependency records,
+their fingerprints, publication identities and parent closures, metadata limits,
+checksums and downloaded-byte authentication remain enforced. Final publication
+validation still runs, including external evidence and parent evidence; its
+workspace portion uses the explicit immutable identity contract. Initial capture
+of individual dependency evidence is not eliminated.
+
+## Existing tiers and the changes
+
+| Tier | Behavior in this patch |
+| --- | --- |
+| T1: retained Skyframe values | Values remain in the existing graph. Previously restored values still pass per-command dependency validation, with eligible workspace filesystem checks skipped in immutable mode. |
+| T2: local sealed publications | Lookup tries admitted local candidate roots first. Publication leases, validation and parent closure remain unchanged. |
+| T3: remote publications | Discovery and preparation begin only after a read exhausts local candidates. Remote candidates then use the existing authenticated demand reader and retained chunk cache. |
+
+`LocalSkycacheModule` no longer eagerly prepares remote publications before the
+build needs them. `LocalCacheSession` discovers on blob lookup misses and on
+exhaustion of analysis candidates, so rejecting a stale local analysis proof can
+still reach a remote revision. Discovery is attempted once per command, preserving
+the existing remote preparation retry policy. Failure is an ordinary cache miss;
+local and private values remain usable. Disabled candidate reads do not discover
+remote publications. Write deduplication does not initiate discovery or RPCs.
+
+Admitted candidate roots are ordered deterministically by local/remote tier and
+publication ID within each tier. This is not source-revision ranking. Private and
+already pinned shared writes retain their existing precedence. Publication
+identity, closure admission and dependency validation are not replaced by tier
+ordering.
+
+Remote bytes retained on disk remain a chunk cache. This patch does not promote a
+partially downloaded remote publication to a complete local publication or create
+an offline catalog. A later command can still require remote catalog access even
+when its blob bytes are retained locally.
+
+`SelectedEntrySerializer` filters restored roots before package bookkeeping and
+batch upload preparation, and returns immediately for an empty remaining
+selection. Locally computed selected children remain eligible for publication;
+restored dependency fingerprints remain available to them. The previous code
+already skipped restored nodes during dependency traversal and upload, so the
+incremental saving is earlier bookkeeping/scheduling avoidance. This is not a new
+elimination of whole-graph serialization. Ordinary locally computed retained
+values are not newly tracked as already persisted by this patch. Existing byte
+deduplication and the zero-new-writes publication skip remain.
+
+## Deliberately deferred
+
+The client ID remains command-specific. Reusing one stable client ID would skip
+the retained-value validation stage entirely, but that stage also imports used
+parent publications and consumed witnesses into the current session. Omitting
+that handoff can lose the evidence needed to publish new values that depend on
+retained values. A future T1 shortcut must preserve this provenance before it can
+skip the stage safely.
+
+Git-diff selective invalidation, generic `BzlLoadValue` caching, complete graph
+restoration with reverse edges, and full remote-to-local publication promotion
+are outside this patch. Source changes initially use a new immutable identity or
+the existing mutable validator rather than a new dependency invalidation index.
+
+## Apply and reproduce
+
+Apply the separately supplied patch to the base commit in an isolated checkout.
+For example, from the fork repository, substituting the actual patch path:
+
+```bash
+git worktree add --detach /tmp/bazel-tiered-review a23ae68903f03f08ce83dcf1be85cdff54bb1eeb
+cd /tmp/bazel-tiered-review
+git apply --check /path/to/skycache-tiered-basic.patch
+git apply /path/to/skycache-tiered-basic.patch
+git diff --check
+bazel --output_base=/tmp/ob-tiered-build build //src:bazel-dev
+cp bazel-bin/src/bazel-dev /tmp/bazel-tiered
+```
+
+Run the focused tests with the repository's supported bootstrap Bazel:
+
+```bash
+bazel --output_base=/tmp/ob-tiered-build test \
+  //src/test/java/com/google/devtools/build/lib/skyframe/serialization/local:LocalInputSnapshotTest \
+  //src/test/java/com/google/devtools/build/lib/skyframe/serialization/local:LocalCacheSessionTest \
+  //src/test/java/com/google/devtools/build/lib/skyframe/serialization/local:RemoteSkyCacheTest \
+  //src/test/java/com/google/devtools/build/lib/skyframe/serialization/analysis:SelectedEntrySerializerTest \
+  //src/test/java/com/google/devtools/build/lib/skyframe/serialization/analysis:AnalysisCacheInvalidatorTest
+```
+
+For workload runs, use `/tmp/bazel-tiered`, a separate runtime output base and a
+local SkyCache directory outside the source workspace. Add the new immutable
+workspace flag only when the surrounding environment enforces its contract. Keep
+the target set, build configuration, binary, ordinary action cache and output
+download policy identical between comparison arms. Use the existing
+[remote MVP setup](../skycache-remote-mvp.md) for remote service configuration.
+
+Before drawing performance conclusions, check these cases:
+
+1. **Warm T1:** repeat the command in the same server. Verify outputs and retained
+   values, and measure the remaining dependency-validation/provenance work.
+2. **Warm T2:** start a fresh server with the same local publications. Verify local
+   restoration and zero remote discovery when local candidates satisfy reads.
+3. **Remote miss path:** use empty local publications and a seeded remote service.
+   Verify on-demand discovery, output equivalence and fallback after remote failure.
+4. **Retained remote bytes:** repeat with retained chunks; report catalog requests
+   separately from downloaded blob bytes. Do not describe this as offline T2.
+5. **Identity and evidence:** change the snapshot identity and external inputs in
+   separate cases. Verify namespace isolation and normal external invalidation.
+6. **Publication:** add a newly computed target that depends on retained values.
+   Verify parent closure, final validation and later restore of the resulting child.
+
+Record wall time, CPU, peak memory, discovery/RPC counts, bytes transferred,
+restored/local hit counts and publication outcomes separately. Compare immutable
+mode against the same patched binary with the flag empty; also retain an
+unmodified-base control when attributing the tier/serializer changes. Actual test
+and workload results belong with the final patch handoff; the checklist above is
+not evidence that those runs passed.
